	"_comment": "Svenska. Suffixed definite articles: gram.article.definite_suffix is ONE blanket rule per gender for the whole language (fil→filen, paket→paketet; plural filer→filerna via +na on the authored plural), not a per-noun override. Swedish truncates the suffix to just -n/-t in TWO shapes the blanket rule cannot see: (1) any stem ending in a stressed vowel (meddelande, beroende, kategori — the -a/-e/-i class takes -n/-t only, e.g. kategorin not kategorien), and (2) common nouns ending in the unstressed -er/-el/-en syllable (server → servern, not serveren). meddelande (message), beroende (dependency), kategori (category) and server (server) are INTENTIONALLY kept as natural vocabulary for done/progress/count/fail composition but are EXCLUDED from definite-suffix reliance — never asserted through DefinitePhrase in the test file. fel, paket, barn, arkiv, objekt and problem are neuter fifth-declension nouns with an invariant (zero) plural, also excluded from the plural-suffix path (they take -en, not -na). Swedish problem→problem (invariant) is a genuine divergence from Danish problem→problemer (regular -er plural) — not a copy-paste leak, checked deliberately. Participles agree with a NEUTER subject only: agreement.participle.n strips -d and adds -t (raderad → raderat), so common stays the base/unmarked form and only neuter transforms — every verb below was chosen from the weak -ad (1st conjugation) or -d/-t (2nd conjugation) classes specifically so the rule derives it; skriva (write) is the one required STRONG verb (skriven/skrivet) whose participle the rule cannot derive, kept for vocabulary honesty but never asserted with a neuter subject. The gerund slot holds the PRESENT TENSE, matching the real UI progress convention ('Raderar...'); köra is the sole bare-stem present (kör, no -r) because its stem already ends in r. Borrowed dev verbs (pusha, fixa, committa, mergea) follow the standard Swedish loanword conjugation (-a infinitive, first conjugation); committa and mergea are the least certain of the set, included on reasonable confidence from casual Swedish tech writing.",
